russia and ukraine united in rivalry  in the interview kremlin critic and  former oligarch michael korkowski  let's start with the situation in  ukraine  at the recent d-day commemorations in  france  there were informal meetings between u.s  president barack obama  russia's vladimir putin  and petro poroshenko the newly elected  ukrainian president the german polish  and russian foreign ministers also met  in saint petersburg the west sees this  as a step towards possible rapprochement  what's your assessment  i'm convinced there must be negotiations  the situation in ukraine is serious  enough  but president putin has also maneuvered  himself into a most delicate and  complicated predicament  he  is there any possibility that the  kremlin can somehow come to terms with  the new government in kiev  or will the threat of draconian  sanctions somehow force russia's hand  there's no doubt that he has a whole  series of measures that he wants to  impose on ukraine  and if kiev accept them putin will put  an end to some of the pressures he's  been exerting  might help to correct putin's demands  with the result of an acceptable outcome  for both sides  but i'm not 100 convinced that sanctions  are the best motivators  if ukraine really desires freedom and  sovereignty  it must be willing to fight for them  the russian president's overriding goal  is pursuing the vision of a eurasian  union his response to the european union  a kind of counterbalance  and to make the dream a reality putin  needs ukraine or at least part of it  is that his prime motivation  the problem is that his strategy for  ukraine has spawned massive negative  consequences for russia uh  russia is a very attractive country  in eurasia it's the recognized leader in  terms of technology and science  russia should exploit these advantages  we could have formed alliances with our  neighbours that would strengthen all  sides  economic relations are fairly solid with  most eurasian nations  but that's mostly due to the sheer lack  of alternatives  but since ukraine  diplomatic relations with these  countries have deteriorated drastically  tensions are high throughout the region  and mistrust has grown exponentially  mistrust both of russia and ethnic  russians who live in those countries  prime examples are the baltic states  this is directly tied to putin's  policies  it may sound unfair but my opinion is  justified  vladimir putin has made broad promises  but has delivered very little  he has duped millions of russians  what about crimea  it's clear russia won't hand it back to  ukraine and the west refuses to  recognize the annexation  will crimea remain a thorn in the side  of relations between russia and the  western alliance  i'm convinced that russia and ukraine  must find a way to work together  and in relations with europe  i want to hear your opinion about the  clash of cultures  and about the apparent rejection of the  western lifestyle  i'm talking about tolerance of  alternative sexuality freedom of speech  individuality and egotism  can russian society accept these values  russians are every bit as much  individualists as the citizens of  western europe  can't go so far as to say that a  collective spirit is far more widespread  in the west  that is precisely the problem we  russians have  we simply can't unite even when it comes  to finding solutions to contentious  issues  it is vitally important to formulate  alliances  independent of government power brokers  seamlessly into western society with  ease  you'll find no russian china towns no  russian districts in western cities  there are so many russians who believe  in themselves in their ability to  compete and in their inherent strengths  they understand that russia is a part of  europe and they also recognize their  status as equals with europe  russia will not tolerate being treated  as a small child  russian culture is closely tied with  europe  without russian influence european  culture would be entirely different  you've also said that the vast majority  of russians are afraid to speak their  minds what can be done to combat the  government-run media  at the moment we don't have too many  alternatives  it's vital that our citizens are well  informed  and putin's regime understands that it  can only retain control of important  information  when that control is exerted  russian television is completely under  government  but print media is increasingly under  pressure as well  and there have been attempts to regulate  the internet too  much  let's say it builds a functioning  vibrant democracy based on law and order  with u.s and european support  how would that affect russia  would it be seen as an opportunity or a  threat  it would be wonderful to see a  prosperous and satisfied ukraine  especially since we have such close ties  it might function as a paradigm a  precedent  people so close to us in terms of  language and culture that have achieved  success  building the foundations of democracy  that outcome could truly provide a  blueprint for similar renewal in russia  pride  i want to see  thank you
 

